::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.8
Question:
Two circles have radii 5 cm and 15 cm. Which transformation proves the circles are similar?
{
=A. A dilation with scale factor 3, possibly after a translation#The radius ratio is 15 divided by 5, so a dilation with scale factor 3 maps one circle size to the other.
~B. A reflection only#A reflection preserves size and cannot change a radius from 5 cm to 15 cm.
~C. A translation only#A translation changes location but not size.
~D. No transformation can compare the circles#A dilation can compare circles with different radii.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
An inscribed angle intercepts an arc measuring 104°. What is the angle measure?
{
~A. 104°#That would match a central angle, not an inscribed angle.
=B. 52°#An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc, so 104° divided by 2 is 52°.
~C. 208°#This doubles the arc instead of halving it.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is given, so the angle can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.6
Question:
A quadrilateral is inscribed in a circle. One angle measures 118°. What is the measure of its opposite angle?
{
~A. 118°#Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral are not necessarily congruent.
~B. 90°#A right angle is not guaranteed by the given information.
=C. 62°#Opposite angles in an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary, so 180° - 118° = 62°.
~D. 242°#This adds to 360°, but opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
A tangent from an exterior point has length t. A secant from the same point has external segment 4 and whole length 25. What is t?
{
~A. 29#Adding the external segment and whole secant does not use the tangent-secant theorem.
~B. 100#This is t^2, not t.
~C. 21#Subtracting the external segment from the whole secant does not find the tangent length.
=D. 10#Use t^2 = external segment times whole secant = 4 times 25 = 100, so t = 10.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.1
Question:
Which construction locates the center of the circumscribed circle of a triangle?
{
=A. Construct the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle sides.#The perpendicular bisectors meet at the circumcenter, the center of the circle passing through all vertices.
~B. Construct the angle bisectors of the triangle.#Angle bisectors locate the incenter, not the circumcenter.
~C. Draw one median of the triangle.#A single median does not locate the circumscribed-circle center.
~D. Draw a tangent from one vertex.#A tangent construction does not locate the triangle's circumcenter.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.2
Question:
A regular hexagon is inscribed in a circle. Which construction idea supports equal side lengths?
{
~A. Use the diameter as every side.#The diameter is too long to form each side of the hexagon.
=B. Step the radius around the circle to create six equal chords.#For an inscribed regular hexagon, each side can be constructed as a chord equal to the radius.
~C. Choose any six points on the circle.#The points must be equally spaced to form a regular hexagon.
~D. Draw six tangent lines.#Tangent lines touch the circle but do not form inscribed sides.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.3
Question:
A tangent line is constructed from an external point to a circle. What must be true at the point of tangency?
{
~A. The tangent passes through the center of the circle.#A line through the center is not tangent at the circle.
~B. The tangent intersects the circle at two points.#A line that intersects the circle at two points is a secant.
=C. The tangent is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of tangency.#A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point where it touches the circle.
~D. The tangent has the same length as the diameter.#Tangent length is not defined by matching the diameter.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector has radius 9 and central angle 80°. Which expression gives the sector area?
{
~A. 80/360(2pi9)#This expression gives arc length, not sector area.
~B. 2pi9#This is the circumference of the full circle.
~C. pi9^2#This is the area of the full circle, not just the sector.
=D. 80/360(pi9^2)#Sector area uses the central-angle fraction of the full circle area, pir^2.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
A central angle measures 76°. What is the measure of its intercepted arc?
{
=A. 76°#A central angle has the same measure as its intercepted arc.
~B. 38°#Halving applies to an inscribed angle, not a central angle.
~C. 152°#Doubling does not describe the central angle relationship.
~D. Cannot be determined#The central angle measure determines its intercepted arc.
}

::GEO_U05_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U05_Pretest_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 90° arc has radius 12. What is the arc length?
{
~A. 12pi#This is half the circumference, but 90° is one fourth of the circle.
=B. 6pi#Arc length is 90/360(2pi12) = 6pi.
~C. 24pi#This is the full circumference.
~D. 36pi#This multiplies the radius by 3pi and does not use the central-angle fraction.
}

